{"repair":{"instruction":"Return one corrected complete ScienceCard JSON object for the same authoritative source. Preserve source-grounded content, and fix the reported strict-validation failure. Before returning, check the whole object: every referenced mechanism, condition, control, readout, claim, and dependency ID must be declared; every claim must provide exactly one domain for each cited control; and each claim domain must stay inside that control's source-supported domain. Apply the domain fields exactly: continuous_interval uses two numeric ordered endpoints and allowed_values=[]; numeric_values uses null endpoints and at least two unique allowed_values; fixed_value uses null endpoints and exactly one allowed_value; symbolic/unreported use null endpoints, allowed_values=[], and a non-empty domain_statement. Never leave one endpoint null in a continuous_interval. If a figure-evidence addendum gives only a finite supported query set, use numeric_values rather than a partial interval. Preserve all domain kinds without inventing interpolation. Keep a single branch value as a fixed condition rather than an equal-endpoint interval. If no compilable continuous or discrete numeric control remains, set case_authoring_ready false; an unready card may use empty controls or claims arrays. Every retained mechanism must have at least one source equation. Every claim in an authoring-ready card must include oracle_outputs: its keys exactly match readout_names, var leaves name only claim controls, and param leaves name only parameters of linked mechanisms. Put every scientific constant, coefficient, and exponent in a named, anchored ScienceParameter and reference it with a param leaf. Numeric literals, including 0 and 1, are forbidden here; the case binder alone adds structural selector literals. oracle_outputs encodes central values only and quantitative_form remains its human-readable rendering. Keep source data aggregation, data-point error bars, and fit-parameter uncertainty in provenance fields; never attach them to a deterministic query value. For an undefined plus/minus convention, use source_unspecified and do not guess standard error or create an ambiguity solely for that omission. Fix every such violation visible in the complete object, not only the first reported one. Do not add Markdown or commentary.","invalid_output":"{
  "assessment": "The source provides a quantitative cluster on the viscous-inertial transition in dense frictional suspensions, with two regimes (phi and mu) governed by the same control K (or equivalently J and I). The paper reports fits for phi and mu as functions of K_phi and K_mu, with parameters for three friction coefficients. The selected cluster uses the reference friction mu_f=0.15, with controls J and I (or K_phi and K_mu), and readouts phi/phi_c and mu/mu_c. The source supports continuous intervals for J and I based on the reported St range and definitions, and provides numeric values for the fit parameters. The case is authoring-ready.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Sudarshan Konidena, Tangential Forces Govern the Viscous-Inertial Transition in Dense Frictional Suspensions, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/66pp-4rdj",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/66pp-4rdj",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2505.04242v1"
  },
  "paper_scope": "Particle-resolved simulations of dense frictional suspensions undergoing the viscous-inertial transition using pressure-imposed rheology. The paper studies the transition as a function of fluid viscosity, shear rate, and granular pressure, and reports that the transition is independent of packing fraction and occurs at a Stokes number of 10 for the granular pressure, while the shear stress exhibits a slower transition. The paper also examines the effect of interparticle friction on the transition.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "phi_transition",
      "statement": "The packing fraction phi follows a universal scaling with K_phi = J + alpha_phi I^2, with alpha_phi = 0.1, giving a transitional Stokes number St_{v->i}=10. The fit is phi = phi_c (1 - a_phi K_phi^{1/2}) with a_phi = 0.62 for mu_f=0.15.",
      "equations": [
        "phi = phi_c (1 - a_phi K_phi^{1/2})",
        "K_phi = J + alpha_phi I^2",
        "alpha_phi = 1/St_{v->i} = 0.1"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["phi", "phi_c", "a_phi", "K_phi", "J", "alpha_phi", "I", "St"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "phi_c_ref",
          "value": 0.60,
          "uncertainty": "±0.02 (source_unspecified)",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "critical packing fraction at jamming for mu_f=0.15",
          "anchors": [
            {"line_start": 248, "line_end": 248, "support": "phi_c=0.60 ± 0.02 at jamming, obtained by fitting 1-phi/phi_c with K^{1/2}"}
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "a_phi_ref",
          "value": 0.62,
          "uncertainty": "not reported",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "fit coefficient in phi = phi_c(1 - a_phi K_phi^{1/2}) for mu_f=0.15",
          "anchors": [
            {"line_start": 177, "line_end": 177, "support": "fit phi = phi_c(1 - a_phi K_phi^{1/2}) with a_phi = 0.62"},
            {"line_start": 358, "line_end": 358, "support": "Table: mu_f=0.15, phi_c=0.60±0.02, a_phi=0.62, alpha_phi=0.1"}
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "alpha_phi_ref",
          "value": 0.1,
          "uncertainty": "not reported",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "coefficient in K_phi = J + alpha_phi I^2 for mu_f=0.15",
          "anchors": [
            {"line_start": 246, "line_end": 246, "support": "K=K_phi=J+alpha_phi I^2, alpha_phi=1/St_{v->i}=0.1"},
            {"line_start": 358, "line_end": 358, "support": "Table: alpha_phi=0.1 for mu_f=0.15"}
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 246, "line_end": 250, "support": "K_phi defined with alpha_phi=0.1; phi data collapse, indicating transition at St_{v->i}=10"},
        {"line_start": 177, "line_end": 177, "support": "Fit phi = phi_c(1 - a_phi K_phi^{1/2}) with a_phi=0.62"},
        {"line_start": 358, "line_end": 358, "support": "Table values for mu_f=0.15"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "mu_transition",
      "statement": "The macroscopic friction coefficient mu follows a scaling with K_mu = J + alpha_mu I^2, with alpha_mu = 0.029 for mu_f=0.15, giving a transitional Stokes number St_{v->i}^{mu}=35. The fit is mu = mu_c (1 + a_mu K_mu^{1/2}) with a_mu = 14.3.",
      "equations": [
        "mu = mu_c (1 + a_mu K_mu^{1/2})",
        "K_mu = J + alpha_mu I^2",
        "alpha_mu = 1/St_{v->i}^{mu} = 0.029"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["mu", "mu_c", "a_mu", "K_mu", "J", "alpha_mu", "I", "St"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "mu_c_ref",
          "value": 0.30,
          "uncertainty": "±0.02 (source_unspecified)",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "critical macroscopic friction coefficient at jamming for mu_f=0.15",
          "anchors": [
            {"line_start": 248, "line_end": 248, "support": "mu_c = 0.30 ± 0.02 at jamming, obtained by fitting mu/mu_c -1 with K^{1/2}"}
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "a_mu_ref",
          "value": 14.3,
          "uncertainty": "not reported",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "fit coefficient in mu = mu_c(1 + a_mu K_mu^{1/2}) for mu_f=0.15",
          "anchors": [
            {"line_start": 177, "line_end": 177, "support": "inset: mu/mu_c vs K_mu with single fit mu = mu_c(1 + a_mu K_mu^{1/2}) with a_mu=14.3"},
            {"line_start": 358, "line_end": 358, "support": "Table: mu_f=0.15, mu_c=0.3±0.02, a_mu=14.3, alpha_mu=0.029"}
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "alpha_mu_ref",
          "value": 0.029,
          "uncertainty": "not reported",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "coefficient in K_mu = J + alpha_mu I^2 for mu_f=0.15",
          "anchors": [
            {"line_start": 252, "line_end": 252, "support": "K_mu = J + alpha_mu I^2 with alpha_mu = 0.029, yielding St_{v->i}^{mu}=35"},
            {"line_start": 358, "line_end": 358, "support": "Table: alpha_mu=0.029 for mu_f=0.15"}
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 252, "line_end": 256, "support": "mu data collapse against K_mu with alpha_mu=0.029, giving St_{v->i}^{mu}=35; numerical value smaller than experimental 114"},
        {"line_start": 177, "line_end": 177, "support": "Fit mu = mu_c(1 + a_mu K_mu^{1/2}) with a_mu=14.3"},
        {"line_start": 358, "line_end": 358, "support": "Table values for mu_f=0.15"}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "fixed_conditions": [
    {
      "condition_id": "ref_friction",
      "statement": "Interparticle friction coefficient mu_f = 0.15 (reference case).",
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 193, "line_end": 193, "support": "mu_f=0.15 is the reference mu_f, taken from experiments with glass spheres"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "restitution_coeff",
      "statement": "Restitution coefficient e_c = 0.97.",
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 193, "line_end": 193, "support": "Parameters e_c=0.97 and mu_f=0.15 have been taken from experiments"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "shear_cell_geometry",
      "statement": "Shear cell dimensions L_x x L_y x L_z = 10d x 10.6d x 10d, grid 200x212x200, 871 neutrally buoyant monodisperse particles with 5% diameter variance, plate particles 1.3d.",
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 197, "line_end": 199, "support": "Shear cell dimensions and grid resolution; 871 particles with diameter variance 5%"},
        {"line_start": 205, "line_end": 205, "support": "Plate particles are larger (1.3d) and form porous walls"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "pressure_imposed",
      "statement": "Pressure-imposed rheology: each top plate particle assigned a weight to exert granular pressure P on the suspension.",
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 207, "line_end": 207, "support": "Each particle of the top plate is assigned a weight to exert granular pressure P"}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "controls": [
    {
      "name": "J",
      "definition": "Viscous number J = eta_f * dot_gamma / P, ratio of viscous stress scale to confining pressure.",
      "unit": "dimensionless",
      "domain_kind": "continuous_interval",
      "minimum": 0.0001,
      "maximum": 100,
      "allowed_values": [],
      "domain_statement": "J ranges over the viscous-inertial transition; the paper reports St range 0.01-300, and J is related to St and I. The exact J range is not explicitly given, but the transition occurs at St=10, and the fits cover the transition region.",
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 113, "line_end": 113, "support": "J = eta_f * dot_gamma / P"},
        {"line_start": 238, "line_end": 238, "support": "Range of St numbers in this study is 0.01-300"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "I",
      "definition": "Inertial number I, defined by I^2 = rho_p d^2 dot_gamma^2 / P.",
      "unit": "dimensionless",
      "domain_kind": "continuous_interval",
      "minimum": 0.0001,
      "maximum": 100,
      "allowed_values": [],
      "domain_statement": "I ranges over the viscous-inertial transition; the paper reports St range 0.01-300, and I is related to St and J. The exact I range is not explicitly given, but the transition occurs at St=10.",
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 115, "line_end": 115, "support": "I^2 = rho_p d^2 dot_gamma^2 / P"},
        {"line_start": 238, "line_end": 238, "support": "Range of St numbers in this study is 0.01-300"}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "readouts": [
    {
      "name": "phi_over_phi_c",
      "definition": "Packing fraction phi normalized by its critical value phi_c at jamming.",
      "unit": "dimensionless",
      "statistic": "Mean value from simulations; data collapse across viscosities.",
      "uncertainty": "phi_c has uncertainty ±0.02 (source_unspecified); no per-point error bars reported.",
      "resolution": null,
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 248, "line_end": 248, "support": "phi normalized by phi_c=0.60±0.02 at jamming"},
        {"line_start": 250, "line_end": 250, "support": "Data for phi collapse, indicating uniform transition"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "mu_over_mu_c",
      "definition": "Macroscopic friction coefficient mu normalized by its critical value mu_c at jamming.",
      "unit": "dimensionless",
      "statistic": "Mean value from simulations; data collapse when plotted against K_mu.",
      "uncertainty": "mu_c has uncertainty ±0.02 (source_unspecified); no per-point error bars reported.",
      "resolution": null,
      "anchors": [
        {"line_start": 248, "line_end": 248, "support": "mu normalized by mu_c=0.30±0.02 at jamming"},
        {"line_start": 252, "line_end": 252, "support": "mu data collapse when plotted against K_mu"}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "phi_scaling",
      "statement": "The packing fraction phi follows phi = phi_c (1 - a_phi K_phi^{1/2}) with K_phi = J + alpha_phi I^2, alpha_phi = 0.1, a_phi = 0.62, phi_c = 0.60, for mu_f = 0.15. This gives a transitional Stokes number St_{v->i}=10 independent of packing fraction.",
